1: # The actual source-zero native return and its first diagonal flop
2: 
3: Proved as P3K.60 after complete root and independent checks audits,
4: 17 November 2026. This note retains the complete family
5: of RG.1--7, including its ordinary and cap closing arrows. The earlier
6: algebra-only attempt remains in
7: \path{notes/part3_source_zero_double_loop_diagnostic.md}. The new
8: geometric step is DZ.1: an actual collar coordinate arrow closes the
9: native single-star product, and its literal pullback closes the
10: doubled three-curve plumbing. DZ.4 identifies the actual square in
11: the relative diffeomorphism group. No individual source-zero class
12: is assigned.
13: 
14: The source, scalar and cap data are
15: \[
16:  Y^2=X^3+(1-2v)X^2+TX+v^2,\quad G=(0,v),\quad
17:  q=T-(v^2-2v),\quad \lambda=v^N\lambda_0,
18: \]
19: \[
20:  (a,b,m)=(1,2,4p)\ \text{or}\ (2,1,4p-1),\quad
21:  N=km,\quad p,k\ge1,\quad n=\pm1 .
22: \]
23: The fan is initially the fan of the two triangles in RG.2a. The
24: physical circle is the original positive phase of \(V\). The opposite
25: diagonal fan is its reflection \(J\mathcal T\). Every source, root,
26: Cartier line, finite character, full \(F_0\), and integer annular
27: cochain is the one in RG/K49. There is no additional logarithmic disc
28: at either colliding finite site.
29: 
30: ## DZ.1. The literal parameter seam on all three edges
31: 
32: Take a small positive \(v_*\), and first parameterize one positive
33: turn by \(v=v_*e^{2\pi i\tau}\), \(0\leq\tau\leq1\). Choose one
34: \(q\)-disc which excludes the other moving nodal value for this
35: compact parameter circle. Its full period maps are
36: \[
37:  h:(Z,V,q)\longmapsto(\kappa qZ,\alpha V,q),\qquad
38:  B:(Z,V,q)\longmapsto(gZ,\eta qV,q),                 \tag{DZ.1}
39: \]
40: where all four coefficients are the actual nowhere-zero holomorphic
41: functions of \(q\) and the cut parameter. Their windings on the
42: double loop, later parameterized by \(v=v_*e^{2i\theta}\), are
43: \[
44:  \begin{pmatrix}\operatorname{wind}\kappa&
45:                  \operatorname{wind}g\\
46:                  \operatorname{wind}\alpha&
47:                  \operatorname{wind}\eta\end{pmatrix}
48:        =2\begin{pmatrix}1&b\\-a&N-2\end{pmatrix}.    \tag{DZ.2}
49: \]
50: The functions themselves, rather than only these integers, are kept
51: below.
52: 
53: Put \(L_0=nk\). The actual forward parameter arrow for one turn is
54: \[
55:  R_{\rm ord}^{(1)}:(Z,V,q)\longmapsto(Z,q^{L_0}V,q). \tag{DZ.3}
56: \]
57: It translates the fan indices by \((0,L_0)\). Applying the actual
58: period \(B^{-L_0}\) returns each chosen cone representative to its
59: original index and leaves the bounded map
60: \[
61:  E_v:(Z,V,q)\longmapsto(g(q,v)^{-L_0}Z,
62:                          \eta(q,v)^{-L_0}V,q).     \tag{DZ.4}
63: \]
64: This is an equality of quotient arrows, not a substitution of a
65: bounded multiplier for the original period.
66: 
67: There are holomorphic-in-\(q\), smooth-in-\(\tau\) logarithms
68: \(G=\log g\), \(H=\log\eta\) on the cut disc times interval. Choose a
69: smooth real function \(\chi\), zero on an initial parameter collar and
70: one on a terminal parameter collar. Define the actual local maps
71: \[
72:  C^{(1)}_\tau:(Z,V,q)\longmapsto
73:        (A_\tau Z,D_\tau V,q),\qquad
74:  A_\tau=e^{L_0\chi G},\quad D_\tau=e^{L_0\chi H}.    \tag{DZ.5}
75: \]
76: They have explicit inverses obtained by negating the exponents.
77: On the terminal collar they are exactly
78: \((Z,V)\mapsto(g^{L_0}Z,\eta^{L_0}V)\), independently of the chosen logarithm
79: branches. Thus, on the two parameter collars identified by the
80: original forward arrow,
81: \[
82:                      C_{\rm end}E_v=C_{\rm start}=1. \tag{DZ.6}
83: \]
84: This equality holds as a map in \(q\) and the collar parameter, so
85: all parameter and spatial derivatives match at the seam. Using only
86: the reference values \(g_*,\eta_*\) would not supply this collar
87: identity; (DZ.5) uses the varying functions.
88: 
89: Every multiplier in (DZ.1) and (DZ.5) depends only on the unchanged
90: coordinates \(q,v\). Consequently \(C^{(1)}_\tau\) commutes literally
91: with \(h,B\), and with the physical circle. It therefore descends
92: to the actual quotient neighbourhood. On the two triangle charts its
93: maps are
94: \[
95:  \begin{split}
96:  T^+:(x_0,x_1,x_2)&\longmapsto
97:        ((AD)^{-1}x_0,Ax_1,Dx_2),\\
98:  T^-:(y_0,y_1,y_2)&\longmapsto
99:        (ADy_0,D^{-1}y_1,A^{-1}y_2).
100:  \end{split}                                          \tag{DZ.7}
101: \]
102: Their three-coordinate products remain \(q\), so the maps extend
103: through both vertices and every toric boundary. In the six named
104: charts \((a_0,b_0,c_0)\), \((u_0,v_0,w_0)\), the three transitions are
105: \[
106:  \begin{array}{c|ccc}
107:  &u_0&v_0&w_0\\ \hline
108:  C_0&\kappa\alpha b_0c_0&\alpha^{-1}a_0b_0&
109:                                       \kappa^{-1}b_0^{-1}\\
110:  C_1&g\eta b_0c_0&\eta^{-1}c_0^{-1}&g^{-1}a_0c_0\\
